<<~ aka lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language>>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Prism

The ''lens-harness''. The node points it at a piece of //existing// content --- an artifact, a transcript, another agent's output, a selection --- and refracts that content through one or more declared lenses, returning a composed ''reading''.

A prism holds the content ''fixed'' and varies the ''viewpoint''. It generates nothing; it bends what already exists through a chosen angle and reveals the structure latent in it. One content enters; a spectrum of perspective-readings leaves, and the bands compose rather than collide --- the instrument of honest plurality, elevating no single band to the whole.

The node summons it. It never rides a turn by default.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/operation>>

!! The Operation

Three moves, in order:

# ''Reference, not copy.'' Prism names the target by `lar:` address, file path, or inline selection --- it points the way `aim` points. The content stays where it lives.
# ''Apply the declared lenses.'' Each lens runs as an independent pass over the referenced span.
# ''Compose as layers.'' Each lens writes its own overlay anchored to the same content; the result reads as the ''union'' of overlays, not a destructive rewrite. Passes run order-independent unless a lens declares it depends on another's output.

The product earns a clean word: a ''reading'' --- a lensed interpretation of a reference. Not a //parse// (which recovers one canonical structure against one grammar), and not bare //annotation// (marginalia). Prism varies the lens; a parse fixes the grammar.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lenses>>

!! The Lenses It Refracts Through

Prism supplies the ''target slot'' the self-applied lenses lack, and reuses the `-> target` aim that `conformance` already carries:

* ''`conformance`'' --- reads the OODA-HA decision-loop band: where the content's loop ran, skipped, or never closed. (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/conformance`)
* ''`syad`'' --- refracts the content into standpoint bands: the five perspectives read the same content, each measuring within its own frame. (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/the-syad-perspectives`)
* ''`mu`'' --- reads the content through the four tools as cognitive facets. (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/the-four-tools`)

A lens reads its tool-bind through the `syad` `/` grammar (`syad=🏛️/*!`); each lens also runs alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/invocation>>

!! Invocation

Declare the lenses, then delegate `->` to the target:

```text
<<~ prism conformance -> lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/some/target/content>>       # single lens, addressed
<<~ prism syad=🏛️ 🗡️ conformance -> lar:///draft.fix.lands>>          # composed spectrum
<<~ prism mu=*! -> "<pasted or quoted content>">>                     # a tool facet, inline
```

The lens list rides left of the `->`; the referenced target rides right. A target that resolves to the node's own current turn reads as a degraded invocation --- Prism reads //other// content, never the node's own live output (kin to the `conformance` rule).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reading>>

!! The Reading

Prism returns the composed overlay: each declared band, what it found, anchored to the same fixed content. Plain glyphs name what the lenses //found// --- the forward `->` declaration prefix stays reserved for the node's own loop.

```text
prism syad=🏛️ 🗡️ conformance -> lar:///draft.fix.lands:
  🏛️ Philosopher  the claim holds where the import graph shows; unsupported where it asserts "cleaner"
  🗡️ Satirist     the fix knifes the symptom, not the cycle --- wrong target, landed anyway
  ↺ conformance   loop snapped at Orient; Act ran before the cause got read; Aftermath never closed
  reading: three bands, one content --- the draft fixes a symptom without orienting.
```

Each band carries complementary signal; the reading composes them into a fuller view than any single lens holds. The overlay feeds the node's //next// decision --- it orients forward.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/lineage>>

!! Lineage

Prism supersedes the old `--parse` language --- a content-decomposition tool that split input text into `lar:` coordinates by Aperture grain and "never answered content." That tool ran a true ''parse'': one input, one canonical decomposition against one grammar.

Prism keeps the worthwhile half --- //reference a piece of content and read its structure// --- and drops the rest: it varies the lens instead of fixing one grammar, composes layered readings instead of one tree, and reads //existing referenced// content instead of decomposing fresh input.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

